<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  High-contrast overlay (§16 accessibility).

  Applied last, after the palette / dark flavour / density overlays, so it can
  strengthen whatever those chose. It is deliberately narrow: it does not
  restate the accent colours, because doing so would fight every palette and
  need one variant per palette. Instead it works on the parts of the design
  that carry contrast regardless of hue - text, borders and dividers.

    Calm Precision leans on low-contrast greys and hairline borders. That is a
  deliberate aesthetic, but it is exactly what fails WCAG for a user with low
  vision, and it is the first thing to break in sunlight. This overlay trades
  the softness away. Measured against white, sRGB relative luminance:

    * secondary text (colorOnSurfaceVariant) goes from sf_neutral_40 at
      6.57:1 to sf_neutral_20 at 13.21:1, clearing AAA (7:1) for body sizes
      rather than only AA
    * card borders are the real failure. WCAG needs 3:1 for a meaningful UI
      boundary; the default outlineVariant sf_neutral_90 manages 1.29:1, so
      the card edge is decorative and effectively invisible to a low-vision
      user. sf_neutral_50 raises it to 4.59:1
    * borders also double from 1dp to 2dp, so the edge survives glare and
      does not depend on a single hairline row of pixels

  The colours here are neutral-ramp references, not literals, so the mono
  palette and dynamic colour both still work underneath.
-->
<resources>

    <style name="ThemeOverlay.SuperFlow.HighContrast" parent="">
        <!-- Text to the extreme of the ramp. sf_neutral_0 is pure black,
             which is correct here: this overlay is not trying to be calm. -->
        <item name="colorOnSurface">@color/sf_neutral_0</item>
        <item name="colorOnBackground">@color/sf_neutral_0</item>
        <!-- The biggest single win: secondary text stops being grey. -->
        <item name="colorOnSurfaceVariant">@color/sf_neutral_20</item>

        <!-- Borders become structural rather than decorative. -->
        <item name="colorOutline">@color/sf_neutral_30</item>
        <item name="colorOutlineVariant">@color/sf_neutral_50</item>
        <item name="sfDividerColor">@color/sf_neutral_50</item>
        <item name="sfCardStrokeWidth">2dp</item>

        <!-- Surfaces flatten to pure white so the tonal steps between
             background, surface and surface-variant do not eat the gain. -->
        <item name="colorSurface">@color/sf_neutral_100</item>
        <item name="android:colorBackground">@color/sf_neutral_100</item>

        <!-- State colours that were intentionally muted need to be legible
             as text, not just as fills. -->
        <item name="sfStateMissed">@color/sf_neutral_30</item>
        <item name="sfStateSkipped">@color/sf_neutral_40</item>
    </style>
</resources>
